WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.490
JENNY BROWN: This week, we're
talking about Elastifile,

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.630
learning GKE, and BigQuery.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:07.230
I'm Jenny Brown, and welcome
to "This Week in Cloud," where

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:10.380
we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.970
Earlier this year, we launched
Elastifile File Service on GCP,

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.140
a fully-managed
version of Elastifile

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.730
integrated with Google Cloud.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.620
So what's going to
change with the intent

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.240
to acquire Elastifile?

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.750
Cloud file storage is set
to become even smarter.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.510
See exactly how
on the Cloud blog.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.490
The demand for running
containerized workloads

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.620
in the cloud keeps
growing, and so has

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.870
the need for people with
the relevant skills.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.390
With our Architecting with
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.550
specialization, you
can gain the skills

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:41.550
to run Kubernetes and deploy
production services on it

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.350
using Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.300
Enroll on your journey today.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.220
Last week, we shared
a solution on how

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.870
to plan your cloud migration.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.180
Today, we have a new
solution specifically

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.720
for migrating to BigQuery from
a data warehouse like Teradata.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.750
This guide includes what to
consider when planning and SQL

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.400
references to get you moving.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.870
Read the details
and get migrating.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.630
If you want to see
one more way BigQuery

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.340
can help you work
with your data,

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.610
check out this blog from
Filipe Hoffa on geolocation.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.890
The sample walks you through
new BigQuery features

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.410
and de-identifying IP addresses.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.780
Protecting your data is
now faster and easier

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:16.990
to do than ever.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:18.917
Thanks for the
breakdown, Felipe.

00:01:18.917 --> 00:01:21.000
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.820
or even see a full list,
check out the link below.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.390
Have any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:26.850
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.970
We'll see you in the cloud.

